error[MODE-1]: a local value cannot be stored into global storage: `src` is local to the caller, but `owned` only receives global values
 --> ../../conformance/MODE/MODE-1-global-store-bad.hir:11:3
  11 |   owned.items = src
     |   ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ 
 --> ../../conformance/MODE/MODE-1-global-store-bad.hir:9:28
   9 | fn put(take dst: Rec, src: Bytes) -> Rec {
     |                            ^^^^^ provided: `src` is a borrowed parameter; it lives in the caller's region
 --> ../../conformance/MODE/MODE-1-global-store-bad.hir:10:11
  10 |   let mut owned = take dst
     |           ^^^^^ required: `owned` is declared without `in owned`, so it receives only global values
 --> ../../conformance/MODE/MODE-1-global-store-bad.hir:12:3
  12 |   take owned
     |   ^^^^^^^^^^ this also fails here for the same cause (MODE-1)
 --> ../../conformance/MODE/MODE-1-global-store-bad.hir:9:38
   9 | fn put(take dst: Rec, src: Bytes) -> Rec {
     |                                      ^^^ required: the result names no `in ..` source, so it is global (MODE-7)
  = help: 1. declare the parameter `take src` (no cost; callers write `take src`, which moves their value into this call)
  = help: 2. `unique src` (deep copy: allocates, costs O(size), and is not allowed under `@noalloc`)
  = note: `copy src` does not help: a copy shares the blocks of `src`, which stay local
  = note: this also fails: MODE-1: this operation requires a global value: `src` is local to the caller, but the result must be global
error[MODE-7]: this use needs an owned value; write `copy` to share it: the value is local, but this needs an owned value
 --> ../../conformance/MODE/MODE-1-global-store-bad.hir:11:17
  11 |   owned.items = src
     |                 ^^^ 
 --> ../../conformance/MODE/MODE-1-global-store-bad.hir:11:17
  11 |   owned.items = src
     |                 ^^^ provided: this value is local here
 --> ../../conformance/MODE/MODE-1-global-store-bad.hir:11:17
  11 |   owned.items = src
     |                 ^^^ required: taking moves the value out of its owner
  = help: 1. write `copy src` here (no deep copy: it shares `src` by a count; needed because `src` is borrowed at this use: it is a part, an element, borrowed storage, or used again later)
  = help: share the value by a count (replace with "copy ")
 --> ../../conformance/MODE/MODE-1-global-store-bad.hir:11:17
  11 |   owned.items = src
     |                 ^ suggested replacement
